---
import Site from "../../layouts/Site.astro";
import { odds } from "../../lib/odds";
import { states, counties } from "../../lib/data";
const unvStates = states.filter((s) => s.unverified.length).length;
const unvCounties = counties.filter((c) => c.unverified.length).length;
---
<Site title="How we check" description="Where Fair Claim's facts come from and how we keep them current.">
  <div class="narrow">
    <p class="eyebrow">Methods</p>
    <h1>How we check</h1>
    <p class="lede">Small claims rules are simple to state and easy to get wrong. Here's how we keep ours honest.</p>

    <h2>Every fact has a source and a date</h2>
    <p>
      Each rule on this site comes from the law itself (a statute or court rule) or the court system's own website or
      forms. We link to it and show the date we last checked. We don't rely on other websites' state-by-state charts:
      many of them are out of date and contradict each other.
    </p>
    <p>
      When we've only found a fact on a secondary source, or official sources disagree, we still show our best
      reading but mark it <span class="badge unverified">unverified</span> until we confirm it.
    </p>
    <p class="hint">
      Right now: {states.length} states and DC loaded, {unvStates} with at least one unverified item;
      {counties.length} California counties, {unvCounties} with at least one unverified item.
    </p>

    <h2>How we keep it current</h2>
    <p>
      Limits and fees change through new laws, usually effective January 1 or July 1. We re-check every state each
      year after legislative sessions end and before those dates, and we re-check any state as soon as we hear of a
      change. If you spot an error, tell us and we'll fix it and note the date.
    </p>

    <h2 id="collecting">What the research says about getting paid</h2>
    {odds.items.length === 0 ? (
      <p>Being compiled.</p>
    ) : (
      <ul>
        {odds.items.map((e) => (
          <li>
            <strong>{e.stat}</strong> — {e.text}{" "}
            <span class="cite">{e.unverified && <span class="badge unverified">unverified</span>}<a href={e.url}>{e.sourceLabel}</a></span>
          </li>
        ))}
      </ul>
    )}

    <h2>Information, not advice</h2>
    <p>
      Fair Claim explains how small claims court works in general and in your state. It can't tell you whether you'll
      win, and it isn't a lawyer. For advice about your own case, California's free small claims advisors, your court's
      self-help center, and local legal aid organizations can help.
    </p>
  </div>
</Site>
